<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Separable Differential Equations</title>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Over time, a sample of a radioactive material
        will decay at a rate proportional to its mass.
        If we call <m>M(t)</m> the mass of the
        radioactive substance, we can express this
        fact by
        <md>
          \frac{dM}{dt}=-kM.
        </md>
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the general solution of the differential
          equation.  (Your solution will depend on the
          constant of proportionality <m>k</m>.)
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="28">
        <p>
          Separating variables: <m>\frac{dM}{M} =
          -k\,dt</m>, so <m>\ln|M| = -kt + C</m> and
          <md>
            M = Ae^{-kt}
          </md>
          for any constant <m>A</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now find the solution where the initial mass
          is <m>M(0) = M_0</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="28">
        <p>
          <m>M(0) = A = M_0</m>, so
          <m>M(t) = M_0 e^{-kt}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The half-life of a radioactive substance is
          the amount of time required for half the mass
          to decay.  Carbon-14 has a half-life of 5730
          years.  What is the value of <m>k</m> for
          Carbon-14?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="28">
        <p>
          <m>M_0 e^{-5730k} = \frac{M_0}{2}</m>, so
          <m>e^{-5730k} = \frac12</m> and
          <md>
            k = \frac{\ln 2}{5730} \approx 0.000121
          </md>
          per year.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          If the mass of a Carbon-14 sample is
          initially 20 grams, how long does it take
          until there are only 5 grams left?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="28">
        <p>
          <m>20e^{-kt} = 5</m> gives
          <m>e^{-kt} = \frac{1}{4}</m>, so
          <md>
            t = \frac{\ln 4}{k} = 2 \cdot 5730
            = 11460
          </md>
          years (exactly two half-lives:
          20g <m>\to</m> 10g <m>\to</m> 5g).
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Suppose you have an investment account that
        earns interest at a rate of 7% compounded
        continuously.  Call <m>M(t)</m> the amount of
        money in the account.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          This means that <m>M</m> changes at the rate
          of 7% of <m>M</m>.  Write this fact as a
          differential equation that describes how the
          amount of money changes.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="28">
        <p>
          <m>\frac{dM}{dt} = 0.07M</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now suppose that you withdraw money at the
          rate of $10,000 per year from the account.
          How does this change your differential
          equation?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="28">
        <p>
          <m>\frac{dM}{dt} = 0.07M - 10000</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the general solution to this
          differential equation.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="28">
        <p>
          Separating variables:
          <m>\frac{dM}{0.07M-10000} = dt</m>, so
          <md>
            M(t) = \frac{10000}{0.07} + Ce^{0.07t}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the solution that corresponds to an
          initial value of $100,000 in the account.
          How long does it take until all the money
          has been withdrawn?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="28">
        <p>
          <m>M(0) = \frac{10000}{0.07} + C
          = 100000</m> gives
          <m>C = 100000 - \frac{10000}{0.07}
          \approx -42857</m>.  Setting
          <m>M(t) = 0</m>:
          <md>
            t = \frac{\ln(10/3)}{0.07}
            \approx 17.2
          </md>
          years.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the smallest amount of money you
          would need to live off the interest
          indefinitely?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="28">
        <p>
          The equilibrium solution is
          <m>M^* = \frac{10000}{0.07} \approx
          \$142{,}857</m>.  At this amount, interest
          earned exactly equals withdrawals, so the
          account balance never changes.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        When a drug is present in a patient's
        bloodstream, it is typically absorbed by the
        body at a rate proportional to the amount in
        the bloodstream.  Let <m>Q(t)</m> be the
        amount of a drug in a patient's bloodstream.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write a differential equation that expresses
          the fact that the rate at which the drug
          leaves the bloodstream is proportional to
          the amount in the bloodstream.  Use <m>k</m>
          as the constant of proportionality.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="28">
        <p>
          <m>\frac{dQ}{dt} = -kQ</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the solution to the differential
          equation with an initial amount
          <m>Q(0) = Q_0</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="28">
        <p>
          <m>Q(t) = Q_0 e^{-kt}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          If the half-life of a particular drug is
          10 hours, find the constant <m>k</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="28">
        <p>
          <m>Q_0 e^{-10k} = \frac{Q_0}{2}</m>, so
          <m>k = \frac{\ln 2}{10} \approx 0.0693</m>
          per hour.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now suppose that a drug is being given
          intravenously at the rate of 2 milligrams
          per hour.  Write a differential equation
          that describes the rate of change of
          <m>Q</m>.  (Just as we have seen before,
          the rate of change is the rate at which the
          drug enters the bloodstream minus the rate
          at which it leaves.)
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="28">
        <p>
          <m>\frac{dQ}{dt} = 2 - kQ</m>, where
          <m>k = \frac{\ln 2}{10}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Assuming that the drug is not initially
          present in the patient, solve the
          differential equation for <m>Q</m> and
          explain what happens after a long time.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="28">
        <p>
          Separating variables with <m>Q(0) = 0</m>:
          <md>
            Q(t) = \frac{2}{k}\left(1-e^{-kt}\right).
          </md>
          As <m>t\to\infty</m>, <m>Q \to \frac{2}{k}
          = \frac{20}{\ln 2} \approx 28.9</m> mg.
          The drug level approaches a steady state
          where the rate of absorption equals the
          rate of infusion.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
